  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "a0",
      "pos": 1,
      "text": "John Rawls described the method of reflective equilibrium in A Theory of Justice.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a1",
      "pos": 2,
      "text": "Ethicists adjudicate between moral theories by testing general principles against intuitions about particular cases.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a2",
      "pos": 3,
      "text": "Consequentialism holds that the moral status of an act depends solely on its outcomes.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a3",
      "pos": 4,
      "text": "On the consequentialist view, nothing other than an act's results determines whether it is right.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a4",
      "pos": 5,
      "text": "Act utilitarianism implies that diverting the trolley to save five lives is required.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a5",
      "pos": 6,
      "text": "Immanuel Kant discussed the trolley problem in the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a6",
      "pos": 7,
      "text": "Deontological theories hold that certain acts are forbidden regardless of the outcomes they produce.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a7",
      "pos": 8,
      "text": "The doctrine of double effect treats harms that are merely foreseen as morally distinct from harms that are intended.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a8",
      "pos": 9,
      "text": "A harm foreseen with certainty counts as an intended harm under the doctrine of double effect.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a9",
      "pos": 10,
      "text": "In the footbridge case the one man's death is the means by which the trolley is stopped.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a10",
      "pos": 11,
      "text": "In the switch case the one workman's death is a side effect of diverting the trolley.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a11",
      "pos": 12,
      "text": "Joshua Greene used functional magnetic resonance imaging to study brain activity during trolley dilemmas.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a12",
      "pos": 13,
      "text": "Greene's dual-process theory attributes characteristically deontological judgments to automatic emotional responses.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a13",
      "pos": 14,
      "text": "Most survey respondents judge that pushing a man from a footbridge to stop the trolley is impermissible.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    }
