  "response": "John Rawls's description of the method of reflective equilibrium in A Theory of Justice shows that ethicists adjudicate between moral theories by testing general principles against intuitions about particular cases. The two rival accounts can be stated sharply before any case is brought to bear on them. Consequentialism holds that the moral status of an act depends solely on its outcomes; in other words, nothing other than an act's results determines whether it is right. Precisely because the balance of results is the only thing that counts, act utilitarianism implies that diverting the trolley to save five lives is required.\n\nImmanuel Kant discussed the trolley problem in the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals, and that discussion confirms that deontological theories hold, by contrast with the utilitarian requirement just stated, that certain acts are forbidden regardless of the outcomes they produce. The machinery with which such theories handle hard cases is the doctrine of double effect, which treats harms that are merely foreseen as morally distinct from harms that are intended. A harm foreseen with certainty counts as an intended harm under the doctrine of double effect; this verdict cannot be squared with the doctrine's own separation of the foreseen from the intended, so one of the two must be abandoned.\n\nAdjudication therefore turns on structural differences between variants of the case rather than on the bare body count. At least one of the following two descriptions is correct, and perhaps both hold together: in the footbridge case the one man's death is the means by which the trolley is stopped; in the switch case the one workman's death is a side effect of diverting the trolley. Joshua Greene used functional magnetic resonance imaging to study brain activity during trolley dilemmas, and those scans confirm the dual-process theory's attribution of characteristically deontological judgments to automatic emotional responses.\n\nThe automatic emotional machinery to which characteristically deontological judgments are attributed is what leads most survey respondents to judge that pushing a man from a footbridge to stop the trolley is impermissible. That pattern of response is produced by the instrumental structure of the footbridge variant: it is because the one man's death is the very means by which the runaway trolley is brought to a halt, rather than an unwanted accompaniment of the rescue, that the judgment comes out as it does. The lesson is not that one account simply defeats the other, but that the intuitions on which adjudication depends track the role a harm plays in an agent's reasoning, which is a structure that a purely outcome-based accounting cannot represent. An ethicist who wishes to retain consequentialism must either explain those intuitions away as artefacts of the mechanisms that generate them or revise the principle until it accommodates them, and the choice between those two strategies is where the real work of the debate now lies.",
